    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The structure, texture, composition, and capillary pressure resistance were assessed for shale deformed along a normal fault with 9 m of dip separation. Shale is entrained from a 1.6 m thick source layer into the fault zone and attenuated to 5 cm. A quantitative analysis of shale mineral composition indicates that little material is contributed to the fault rock from the sandstone units that over- and underlie the shale source layer. This finding is in contrast to common predictive models of fault sealing that assume mechanical wear along the fault surfaces. The composition of deformed shale indicates effective mixing of clay- and quartz-rich layers of the shaly source unit by granular flow during shale deformation.\r\n\r\nCapillary displacement pressures of deformed shale are 30% higher compared to the most clay-rich undeformed shale outside the fault and two orders of magnitude higher compared to sand-rich undeformed shale. This increase in sealing capacity is primarily attributed to the development of a planar fabric in deformed shale. Enhanced clay diagenesis likely contributed to the increase in shale sealing capacity. It is concluded that fault seal by shale entrainment involves a variety of structural, textural, and diagenetic processes that require an integrated methodology for a successful prediction of fault sealing capacity.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
